@* One axis on the entry form — and the same component in the settings preview, so what a teacher
   sees and what an admin previews cannot drift apart.

   Three things this component exists to get right:

   1. NESTING. A child group is rendered indented UNDER the value that opened it and labelled with
      that value's name. A single answer opens at most one child group per axis, but the same
      component draws groups three and four levels deep, and in a flat list nobody could tell which
      sub-answer belongs to which parent.

   2. ONE DROPDOWN, ONE ANSWER — every axis, at the team's instruction. The checkbox grid that used
      to appear when MaxSelections != 1 is gone, so the cap an admin configures no longer changes
      the control here; picking always replaces. Nothing here branches on a part of speech or an
      axis name either.

      What the callers owe this: a pick must REPLACE. The server still toggles on an axis that
      allows several (see ClassificationService.SetFeatureAsync), so the station clears whatever
      else that axis holds before recording the new answer — otherwise a second pick would add a
      value this one control cannot show.

   3. NOT-APPLICABLE IS PART OF THE SAME CONTROL, not a second one beside it. A value and «کار
      ناکات» are mutually exclusive, and two independent controls invite a sense that is
      simultaneously answered and declared inapplicable.

   4. THE TEACHER READS A QUESTION, NOT A FIELD NAME. What is printed above the control is
      Axis.Ask — the written question if the settings screen has one («کارەکە چ جۆرێکە؟»), the raw
      technical label only as a fallback. A teacher reading «جۆری کار» does not know what is being
      asked of them, and the answer they pick to get past it is stored as if it were a judgement.

   5. NOTHING IS EVER PRE-SELECTED. Every dropdown opens on «— هەڵبژێرە —». A control that opens
      showing the first real value looks answered while nobody has touched it, and that is how a
      wrong value gets saved silently — the single most expensive kind of bad data here, because it
      is indistinguishable from work. *@

<div class="@IndentClass">

    @* The parent value that opened this group. Without this label the nesting is decoration. *@
    @if (Axis.ParentValueName is { } parent)
    {
        <p class="mb-1 text-11 text-ink-3">
            چونکە <span class="text-on-tint">@parent</span> هەڵبژێردراوە
        </p>
    }

    <div class="mb-2 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
        @if (ShowName)
        {
            @* Axis.Ask, never Axis.Name: the written question if there is one, the bare label only
               when nobody has written one yet. The fallback lives on the DTO so this component and
               the settings preview cannot disagree about which string is shown. *@
            <span class="text-13 text-ink-1">@Axis.Ask</span>
        }

        @if (Axis.IsRetired)
        {
            @* Deactivated in settings, but this sense answered it. Retiring an option hides it from
               NEW entries; it never erases what is already recorded. *@
            <span class="pill pill-muted">ناچالاککراوە</span>
        }

        @if (Axis.MinSelections > 0 || Axis.IsRequired)
        {
            <span class="text-11 text-warn" title="لە کەمووکوڕی وشەکاندا دەردەکەوێت — ڕێگری لە پاشەکەوت ناکات">
                پێویست
            </span>
        }

        @* The «٢ لە ٣ هەڵبژێردراو» counter is gone with the checkboxes: one dropdown holds one
           answer, and it is showing it. *@

        @if (Axis.IsNotApplicable)
        {
            <span class="pill pill-muted">کار ناکات</span>
        }
    </div>

    @* One dropdown, whatever the axis's configured cap. Choosing replaces, and choosing a value
       that opens a further group makes the next dropdown appear directly beneath this one.

       A native <select> rather than the Telerik dropdown the rest of the admin uses, for one
       reason: the station is keyboard-first. Digits ١-٩ pick a value without the mouse, which
       is what makes four thousand senses finishable, and the digit handler on the page needs
       the keystroke to reach it rather than being swallowed by a rich control's own key
       handling. The digit is printed in each option so the shortcut stays discoverable. *@
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
        <select value="@(Axis.SelectedValueId?.ToString() ?? "")"
                @onchange="OnSelectChanged"
                class="field w-auto min-w-[13rem] max-w-full py-2">
            @* The empty prompt option, and it is SELECTED whenever the sense holds no answer —
               explicitly, not by relying on it happening to be first. A dropdown that opens on a
               real value reads as answered by somebody, and nothing downstream can tell that
               apart from a teacher's actual judgement. *@
            <option value="" selected="@(Axis.SelectedValueId is null)">— هەڵبژێرە —</option>

            @foreach (var value in Axis.Values)
            {
                @* SelectedValueId, not "is this value in the list": a sense saved while this axis
                   still took several can hold two, and marking both selected in a one-answer
                   <select> would silently show whichever the browser picked last. It shows the
                   first, which is the one this control will replace. *@
                <option value="@value.ValueId" selected="@(value.ValueId == Axis.SelectedValueId)">
                    @OptionLabel(value)
                </option>
            }
        </select>

        @if (ShowNotApplicable)
        {
            <button type="button" @onclick="() => OnNotApplicable.InvokeAsync(Axis)"
                    class="@(Axis.IsNotApplicable
                             ? "rounded-full border border-line bg-page px-3 py-2 text-xs text-ink-1"
                             : "rounded-full border border-dashed border-line px-3 py-2 text-xs text-ink-3 transition-colors hover:bg-page")">
                کار ناکات
            </button>
        }
    </div>

    @* ── Worked examples ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Muted, beneath the control, and only for the options that actually carry one.

       Beneath rather than inside: a native <option> cannot be styled, so a hint appended to the
       option text would read with the same weight as the answer itself, and the longest hint would
       set the width of the whole dropdown.

       Before a choice is made, every hint that exists is shown — that is the moment they are needed,
       because the teacher is deciding between them. Once an answer is in, only that answer's hint
       stays, so the field settles down instead of carrying a permanent block of help text. *@
    @if (VisibleHints is { Count: > 0 } hints)
    {
        <div class="mt-1.5 space-y-0.5">
            @foreach (var value in hints)
            {
                <p class="text-11 leading-relaxed text-ink-3">
                    <span class="text-ink-2">@value.Name</span> — @value.Hint
                </p>
            }
        </div>
    }

    @if (Axis.IsNotApplicable && !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Axis.Note))
    {
        <p class="mt-1.5 text-xs text-ink-2">@Axis.Note</p>
    }

    @if (Notice is { } notice)
    {
        @* A remote configuration change. INLINE and non-blocking: the field stays on screen with
           whatever was typed into it, and the structural change applies after the next save. *@
        <p class="mt-1.5 flex items-start gap-1.5 text-11 text-warn">
            <Icon Name="alert" Class="mt-0.5 h-3 w-3 shrink-0" />
            @notice
        </p>
    }
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public StationAxisDto Axis { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>Fired with the axis and value the user picked. Toggling is the caller's decision.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<(int AxisId, int ValueId)> OnPick { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public EventCallback<StationAxisDto> OnNotApplicable { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Keyboard hints, shown only on the axis the digits currently address.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowDigits { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The settings preview passes true; it is interactive but writes nothing.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Interactive { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>An inline notice — «ئەم بژاردە لەلایەن ئەدمین گۆڕدرا». Never a modal.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Notice { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether to print the group's own name above its control.
    ///
    /// False when it is the ONLY first-level group, because then the part of speech has already
    /// named the question: under کار, a heading reading «دەمژمێر» above the one dropdown is a label
    /// for something the teacher has just selected, and it earns nothing. Sub-groups always keep
    /// their name — that is what tells you which parent a nested answer belongs to.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowName { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>
    /// «کار ناکات» sits INSIDE the same control as the values, not beside it — a value and
    /// not-applicable are mutually exclusive, and two independent controls invite a sense that is
    /// both answered and declared inapplicable.
    ///
    /// The settings preview writes nothing, so it does not offer the escape hatch: there is no sense
    /// there to declare anything about.
    /// </summary>
    bool ShowNotApplicable => Interactive && OnNotApplicable.HasDelegate;

    int SelectedCount => Axis.SelectedValueIds.Count;

    /// <summary>
    /// The option hints to print under the control: all of them while the axis is unanswered, then
    /// only the chosen options'.
    ///
    /// Empty rather than null-checked at the call site, and capped at six: an axis with twelve
    /// hinted options would push the next question off the screen, and past a handful the hints stop
    /// being a glance and become reading.
    /// </summary>
    List<StationValueDto> VisibleHints =>
        Axis.Values
            .Where(v => !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(v.Hint))
            .Where(v => SelectedCount == 0 || Axis.SelectedValueIds.Contains(v.ValueId))
            .Take(6)
            .ToList();

    /// <summary>
    /// Indent by depth, with a rule on the start edge so the nesting is visible rather than implied.
    /// Capped at four steps: past that the indentation is eating the readable width, and the parent
    /// label is what actually carries the relationship.
    /// </summary>
    string IndentClass => Axis.Depth switch
    {
        0 => "",
        1 => "ms-4 border-s border-line ps-3",
        2 => "ms-8 border-s border-line ps-3",
        3 => "ms-12 border-s border-line ps-3",
        _ => "ms-16 border-s border-line ps-3",
    };

    Task Pick(int valueId) =>
        Interactive ? OnPick.InvokeAsync((Axis.AxisId, valueId)) : Task.CompletedTask;

    /// <summary>
    /// A dropdown change. Picking the blank entry clears the group by re-sending the value that is
    /// already held — the server treats a repeat pick on a single-select axis as a no-op and on a
    /// multi-select one as a toggle, so clearing goes through the same path everything else does
    /// rather than needing a second endpoint.
    /// </summary>
    async Task OnSelectChanged(ChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        if (!Interactive) return;

        var raw = e.Value?.ToString();

        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(raw))
        {
            if (Axis.SelectedValueId is { } held) await Pick(held);
            return;
        }

        if (int.TryParse(raw, out var valueId)) await Pick(valueId);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The option text. Carries the digit shortcut so it stays discoverable inside a dropdown, and a
    /// «‹» marker on values that open a further group — without it there is no way to tell which
    /// choice is a leaf and which one asks another question.
    /// </summary>
    string OptionLabel(StationValueDto value)
    {
        var label = value.Name;

        // Only on the group the digits currently address. Numbering every dropdown at once would
        // print a shortcut next to a value the keystroke does not reach.
        if (ShowDigits && value.Digit > 0) label = $"{value.Digit} · {label}";
        if (value.OpensChildGroup) label += "  ‹";
        if (value.IsRetired) label += "  (ناچالاک)";

        return label;
    }
}
